WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.160
- [Narrator] Judge Amy Coney Barrett

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.940
is expected to be President
Donald Trump's pick

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.970
to fill Ruth Bader Ginsburg's
seat on the US Supreme Court.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.620
He's hoping she'll become the Court's

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.830
all-important sixth conservative vote.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.140
If confirmed by the Senate,
Barrett, at 48 years old,

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.610
could shape the high court for decades.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:22.400
- A president has a
bigger impact on the Court

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.370
the longer the justice he or
she appoints stays in office.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:27.810
- [Narrator] She's long
expressed her affinity

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.910
for the late Justice Antonin Scalia,

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.940
whom she clerked for the
in the Supreme Court,

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:33.990
and his support for originalism,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.740
or interpreting the Constitution

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.240
according to its original meaning.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.590
- Justice Scalia's philosophy
of judging was not only right,

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:44.230
as a matter of the
Constitutional structure,

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.060
but it was also self-disciplined.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.860
- [Narrator] Barrett
returned to her alma mater

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.000
to launch her academic career.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.820
She still teaches law at Notre Dame.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.410
She became well known in
conservative legal circles

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:57.410
and spoke to audiences around the country

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:00.680
about what the Supreme
Court's role should not be

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.470
when it comes to social change.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.110
- When legislative change is slow,

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:06.990
because people increasingly see the Court

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.300
as a political institution,

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.350
when they can't accomplish
things in the legislature,

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:11.480
they try to litigate it

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.007
and then declare a fast
win through the Court

00:01:13.007 --> 00:01:16.310
because they think it's
faster to get it through

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.070
the justices than to
convince their citizens

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:23.050
in the legislative process.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.280
- [Narrator] She offered, as an example,

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.170
the issue of transgender students

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.220
who were seeking access to the restrooms

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.130
that matched their gender identity.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.100
Lawyers for transgender students argued

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.080
that under a 1972 civil rights law

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.630
their clients rights were being violated.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.750
The part of the law
prohibiting discrimination

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.770
in education based on
sex is known as Title IX.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:44.940
- It's a (speaks in
foreign language) question.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.980
When Title IX was enacted,
it's pretty clear that no one,

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.980
including the Congress
that enacted that statute

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.940
would have dreamed of
that result at that time.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.060
Maybe things have changed so
that we should change Title IX,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.010
but it does seem to strain
the text of the statute

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:02.730
to say that Title IX demands it.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.010
- [Narrator] In her 2017

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.020
appellate court confirmation hearings,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.970
Barrett emphasized time and again

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:11.100
that a judge's personal
opinions shouldn't matter.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.340
- I think if I express a personal view

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.160
on any of these matters, it
might give the misimpression

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.520
that my personal view is what would drive

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:19.730
the decision of a case.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:21.230
It would not be.
- No, I stipulate

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:22.730
that that won't happen.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:23.990
- [Narrator] As for her personal view

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:27.780
on landmark Supreme Court
rulings, Barrett wouldn't budge.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.110
Even when those questions
came from Republicans

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.040
who supported her nomination.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.770
- What did you think about the opinion?

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.666
- I wasn't alive when
Griswold came down. (laughs)

00:02:37.666 --> 00:02:39.730
- [John] But what about
when you first read it?

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:42.350
- Well, gosh senator, I think, again,

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.030
whatever I might have thought about it,

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.417
I first read it when I was a law student.

00:02:46.417 --> 00:02:47.250
- [John] Uh-huh.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:48.720
- But whatever I would
have thought about it then

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.060
or whatever I would think
about it today wouldn't matter.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.560
I would put that aside.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.290
- [Narrator] In Griswold
v. Connecticut in 1965,

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.240
the Supreme Court found a state law

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.820
which prevented married couples
from using contraception

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.650
to be unconstitutional.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.630
The Court's opinion recognized
within the Constitution

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.320
a right to privacy.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.300
- Do you believe there's
a right to privacy

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:11.680
under the Constitution?

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.500
- The Court has quite
clearly held that there is.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:17.260
- And do you agree with the Court?

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.320
- Senator Blumenthal, I would
follow all Court precedent

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.620
and whether I agree or disagree
would be beside the point

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.110
to the discharge of my duties,

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.100
if I were confirmed as a judge.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.770
- So you have no personal views

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:31.070
on whether there's a right to
privacy in the Constitution?

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:32.140
- Well senator, I think the question

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.480
is whether I have personal views

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.240
that would be appropriate
to share in this context

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.960
given the misimpression
it could give litigants

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.740
who might appear before
me if I were confirmed.

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:45.170
- [Narrator] Both Democrats
and Republicans asked Barrett

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:47.810
whether she'd seek to
overturn Roe v. Wade.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:51.350
- If confirmed, would you
follow Supreme Court precedent

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.340
involving abortion?

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:54.587
- Absolutely, I would.

00:03:54.587 --> 00:03:57.037
- Do you think Roe v. Wade
was correctly decided?

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:00.970
- Well senator, I'm sorry
that I feel like I can't,

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:02.990
as a nominee, offer an opinion.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.940
Because I don't wanna give the impression

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.530
that I would treat some
precedents as more binding

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:08.820
or more valuable than others.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.930
I would apply Roe and its progeny

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.260
just like I would apply all
Supreme Court precedent.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:14.470
- [Narrator] The Senate confirmed Barrett

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:19.020
to the Seventh Court in
Chicago by a vote of 55 to 43.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:22.220
There, she began to establish
her judicial record.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.570
In 2019, she described an example

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.510
of how she applied her principles

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:30.160
in a case involving Second
Amendment rights for felons.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.440
- After serving all of the arguments,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.560
the historically based arguments
for the Constitutionality

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:40.070
of depriving felons of their
right to possess firearms,

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:44.050
that there was no blanket
authorization to just take guns,

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.330
that the right to possess
a gun away from someone

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:47.450
who'd committed a felony.

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.800
- [Narrator] Progressives
worry that Barrett would create

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.760
a more conservative Supreme Court

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.840
that could block their aspirations.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.290
Barrett has long said
she believes the public,

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:58.070
through their elected representatives,

00:04:58.070 --> 00:04:59.370
rather than the courts,

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.940
should lead the way in social change.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.380
- The who decides question,
just as a personal matter,

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.380
is really important to me

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:12.720
and so I guess I worry a lot, you know,

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.883
about that who decides question,

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:20.780
about our decisions and my voice
kind of getting taken away.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:22.680
- [Narrator] Now Barrett
may herself become

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.158
the one who decides.

00:05:24.158 --> 00:05:26.741
(gentle music)

